# `actuation:` is Agent 0.2.0's actuation domain (domains/actuation/) since the 0.1.0 package that
# declared it was amended there; this package's texts still speak its words, so it binds the label.
@prefix actuation: <http://example.org/orexis/actuation#> .
@prefix mqtt: <http://example.org/orexis/mqtt#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/mqtt#> .
@prefix orexis:   <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .

<http://example.org/orexis/mqtt> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Orexis MQTT binding" ;
    rdfs:comment """One way of reaching things: a broker and named channels on it. Everything here is a VARIANT — a society could speak Modbus to its sensors, or read a GPIO pin with no bus at all, and would swap this module without touching sensing, market or actuation. Note what is NOT here: any capability. Which protocol a device speaks changes nothing an agent must decide, so it earns a binding on the device, not a name in the agent's abilities.""" .

#################  The bus  #################
# In the WORLD, not in the environment: a topic means nothing without the broker it is on,
# and every agent must agree on the bus or they are not in the same society at all. The only
# thing a process is told by its environment is where the belief base is; the belief base
# then says everything else, including this.

:Linking a orexis:Capability ; rdfs:label "Linking" ;
    rdfs:comment "The ability to reach the society over this transport — granted by the FACT of a bus in the world, to every agent, as a sensor grants sensing. The module that holds it owns the connection, hands every message to whoever can handle it, sends for whoever asks, and watches its own session; the kernel has no mailbox (the-kernel-has-no-mailbox)." .

:MessageBus a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Message bus" ;
    rdfs:comment "A broker agents reach each other through. Found by type; when there is more than one, resources name theirs with mqtt:onBus." .

:brokerHost a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :MessageBus ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "broker host" .
:brokerPort a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :MessageBus ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "broker port" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a principal that authenticates by password connects. The boards use this: a device that deep-sleeps between readings would pay a TLS handshake on every wake." .
:brokerTlsPort a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :MessageBus ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "broker TLS port" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a principal that authenticates by CERTIFICATE connects. Optional: a world without it has no mTLS door, and its agents use the password one. Same bus and the same ACL either way — this is how you prove who you are, not which society you are in." .
:onBus a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range :MessageBus ;
    rdfs:label "on bus" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which bus this resource is reachable on. Optional while a society has one." .

#################  Channels  #################

:readingTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "reading topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a sensor PUBLISHES what it read. Several sensors may name the SAME topic: one board carrying two peripherals is one MQTT client with one credential, so it publishes one message and each sensor takes its own value out of it with mqtt:readingPointer." .

:readingPointer a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "reading pointer" ;
    rdfs:comment "WHICH value in the payload is this sensor's, as a JSON Pointer (RFC 6901, April 2013, Standards Track): `/temperature`, `/readings/0/rh`. Absent means `/value`, which is what every single-property device already publishes — so this is invisible until a device reports more than one thing. A POINTER and not a JSONPath (RFC 9535) because a pointer identifies exactly ONE value, which is exactly what a reading is; a query language returns a nodelist and would need a collapse rule invented on top of it. Nothing here says how the bytes became a document — that is the codec, and JSON is the only one. See knowledge/decisions/a-reading-is-one-value-so-it-is-pointed-at.md." .
:commandTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "command topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a device LISTENS for instructions: a cadence for a sensor, a dose for a valve. Only a device that accepts instruction has one." .
:statusTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "status topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where a device REPORTS what it actually did. A valve publishes here after it has opened, so the difference between a command being sent and water having flowed is on the wire rather than assumed. `firmware/pump-valve` has always published one, deriving the name in C as its command topic plus \"/status\"; stating it here is the same correction orexis-firmware made when it found the sensor firmware building its own topics from PLANT_ID." .
:eventTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "event topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where an agent announces something it noticed. Peers may listen; nobody is obliged to." .

#  A device carrying these properties is reached over MQTT; that is what selects the driver
#  the sensing module talks to it through. An agent may hold sensors on different
#  bindings at once and still have ONE attention policy, which is the point of keeping
#  protocol out of the capability.

#################  A channel is a thing, not a string  #################
#
# `mqtt:readingTopic` and the two beside it are LITERALS, and until now the stream each one named
# had no node. That is why nothing could say *this stream is JSON* — the thing the fact is about
# was never declared — and why `_aimed_with` recovers a group by comparing strings. It is the
# same defect the board had before it was a `sosa:Platform`, one level down.
#
# So a channel is DERIVED, never written: the topics are the premise and `rules.ru` mints one
# node per distinct topic. Two sensors naming the same string land on the same node, which is
# the whole point — sharing a stream is a fact about the world, not a coincidence of authoring.

:Channel a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Channel" ;
    rdfs:comment "One named message flow on a bus. Derived from the topics devices state, one node per distinct topic, so it is discovered rather than authored — a world that names a topic twice has one channel and knows it. What it bears is the ENCODING: one channel, one format, which is the claim worth refusing to break and which no sensor-borne fact could make, because a command channel has no sensor at all. Not a member of anything: there is one way to be a channel. Named here rather than in the kernel because its premise is this transport's — a REST binding would derive its own from URLs, and the day that exists is the day this is worth hoisting." .

:channelTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Channel ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "channel topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "The string this channel is named by, carried so that anything holding the node can address it without walking back to whichever device stated it. The node's own IRI is minted from this and is stable because of it: two rules in two packages compute the same IRI from the same topic without either reading the other's conclusions, which is what makes a derived node usable as a join key at all." .

#################  Carrying it — a procedure, and whose it is  #################
#
# A protocol is a `sosa:Procedure` in SOSA's own list of what one may be, so naming it costs
# nothing and buys a place to hang the thing that was previously only implied: SOMEONE turns a
# reading into a message, and it is not the part that produced the reading.

:Publishing a sosa:Procedure ; rdfs:label "MQTT publishing" ;
    rdfs:comment """Connecting to a broker as an authenticated principal and sending on a named channel. Implemented by whatever holds the credential — the BOARD, in every world here, which is why `mc:Microcontroller` is an `ssn:System` and not only a `sosa:Platform`. A KY-015 has never published anything; it clocks out a frame on a wire and the board does the rest.

**The world does not yet say this, and the reason is worth knowing.** `mqtt:readingTopic` sits on the sensors, so `mqtt:publishesOn` derives onto them and the graph reads as though three channels each published for themselves. It is harmless while a board carries one credential and every sensor on it shares one topic — the strings agree because the board is the thing they describe — and it stops being harmless the moment anything asks WHO sent a message. Moving the topic to the board it belongs to is issue #103's neighbourhood; stating the procedure first is what makes the mismatch nameable at all.

Note what is NOT claimed: that publishing is a capability. Which protocol a device speaks changes nothing an agent must decide, and that reasoning is unaltered by giving the protocol a name.""" .

:publishesOn a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range :Channel ;
    rdfs:label "publishes on" ;
    rdfs:comment "This device SENDS on that channel — derived from mqtt:readingTopic or mqtt:statusTopic. The direction lives on the relation rather than on the channel, because a stream is one thing however many parties read it: putting read/write in the channel's identity would split one topic into two nodes the moment a device both sent and listened on it." .

:listensOn a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range :Channel ;
    rdfs:label "listens on" ;
    rdfs:comment "This device RECEIVES on that channel — derived from mqtt:commandTopic. The mirror of mqtt:publishesOn, and together they are the direction a principal's grants already carry: `Principal.grants` has been a set of (read|write, topic) pairs since series-and-bus-isolation, which is a set of directed channels written in the form mosquitto wants." .
